  "text": "Here's the completed function `remove_adjacent_duplicates` that removes consecutive duplicate elements from a list of integers, and it passes all the provided test cases:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]  # Start with the first element\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- The function starts by handling the edge case where the list is empty.\n- It initializes the result list with the first element of the input list.\n- Then, it iterates through the remaining elements.\n- For each element, it checks if it is different from the last element in the result list.\n- If it is different, it adds the element to the result list.\n- This ensures only consecutive duplicates are removed.\n\n### How to Run:\nYou can run this script directly, and it will execute the unit tests and output the results.",
